   "order": 0,
   "era": "First sports watch",
   "kicker": "Generation I · Seikosha",
   "refs": [
    "14041"
   ],
   "blurb": "Hand-wound Seikosha 17-jewel movement in a ≈35 mm steel case, and the only Alpinist to carry the Laurel name. Launched at ¥4,500, per the 1959 SEIKO NEWS cited by Hodinkee Japan.",
   "dispute": "Seiko’s English and Japanese pages give 1959. Seiko Germany’s 2020 release and Seiko India give 1961.",
   "lead": "laurel-alpinist-14041"
  },
  {
   "id": "champion",
   "name": "Champion Alpinist",
   "order": 1,
   "era": "Daini Seikosha",
   "kicker": "Champion line",
   "match": "^J1303|^J1304",
   "blurb": "The Alpinist name moved to the Champion line, with sector-style dials, multi-chamfered lugs and the mountain engraving on the caseback. Several references are recorded, each with its own dial design."
  },
  {
   "id": "champion850",
   "name": "Champion 850 Alpinist",
   "order": 2,
   "era": "Calibre 850",
   "kicker": "Champion line",
   "match": "^J13079|^85899",
   "blurb": "Named after its calibre. ‘Alpinist 850’ appears on the caseback with the mountain logo. The Alpinist name then went quiet for three decades."
  },
  {
   "id": "revival95",
   "name": "Alpinist revival (4S15)",
   "order": 3,
   "era": "The compass arrives",
   "kicker": "Revival · 1995",
   "match": "^SCVF",
   "blurb": "The Alpinist returned in 1995 with the internal rotating compass ring and the second crown at four o’clock, the two features that define it today. Cathedral hands, a red ‘Alpinist’ script and an automatic 4S15 movement.",
   "dispute": "Many sources say 1992; the research found 1992 is the year the 4S calibre family appeared, and 1995 the Alpinist’s return.",
   "lead": "alpinist-scvf009"
  },
  {
   "id": "gmt8f56",
   "name": "Alpinist GMT Perpetual (8F56)",
   "order": 4,
   "era": "High-accuracy quartz",
   "kicker": "Quartz branch",
   "match": "^SBCJ",
   "blurb": "A titanium quartz branch: perpetual calendar, a 24-hour GMT hand and Seiko’s high-accuracy 8F56 calibre (±20 s/year per Seiko’s manual). Includes the 500-piece SSASS edition for mountaineer Ken Noguchi.",
   "dispute": "Launch year reported as both 2002 and 2003."
  },
  {
